 "This compass, made in about 1860, uses the principle of magnetism to indicate direction. When magnetized, the needle is free to move in response to the Earth's magnetic field. The direction of the needle is indicated by the direction of the magnetic field lines, which flow from the North Magnetic Pole towards the South Magnetic Pole. The museum note (left) illustrates the construction of the compass, showing the direction of the magnetic field lines (black arrows) and the direction of the needle (blue arrow) when magnetized. It also provides information about how to use the compass, including the importance of keeping it away from other magnetic fields (lacks information) that might interfere with its accuracy."
